p='nancy_christie';s='lof_christie_2009'
P(p,
'A historian of family, welfare and religion whose work asks how liberal authority was made through everyday institutions. In her 2009 intervention, Christie credits Ian McKay’s framework with connecting cultural and social research while disputing its periodization, statism and property-based account of individualism. She uses Bruce Curtis’s distinction between liberalism and governmentality to seek a more precise account of power. Churches, voluntary associations and households become sites of contested liberal values, including women’s participation as well as exclusion. Her appeal to Habermas’s civil society remains selective: she also points to work complicating the public/private division and insists on sentiment alongside rationalism. Engendering the State supplies one earlier research context; the religion books retain Michael Gauvreau’s coauthorship. The intervention revises the framework’s explanatory reach rather than simply replacing it with another single ideology.',
'Complete 2009 institutional essay re-read from the earlier authorized local receipt, with its paragraph openings used as locators. JSTOR introduction opening and publication records retain earlier limited scopes; full selected books unread. Christie names no particular Curtis text, so that work remains unidentified. Her judgment of McKay and her use of Habermas are attributed, not a fresh reading of their complete texts. Joint authorship and employer-claim hold remain.',
edges=[E(p,'mckay',p,'ian_mckay','Credits the synthesizing value of McKay’s liberal-order framework while disputing its timing, statism and property-centred individualism.',s,'Complete 2009 essay, opening and The first issue; Since liberalism; Probably the most important site. Specific qualified criticism, not rejection of every McKay argument.',kind='critique'),E(p,'curtis','bruce_curtis',p,'Christie uses Curtis’s governmentality distinction to sharpen her criticism of an overly state-centred liberal-order account.',s,'Paragraph beginning Since liberalism was a response explicitly names Curtis. The particular Curtis work is unidentified; no independent reading or sole derivation asserted.'),E(p,'habermas','jurgen_habermas',p,'Christie employs civil society as a resource for investigating families, churches and voluntary associations while complicating the public/private division.',s,'Since liberalism; Probably the most important site; final substantive paragraph. Habermas is named with McNairn; later studies and sentiment qualify the application. Original Habermas texts unread.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Complete essay; local HTML checksum already recorded in initial packet',method='Re-read argument and named resources, separating explicit attribution from bibliographic inference')],holds=['Curtis text unidentified; selected monographs unread in full.'])

p='keith_thomas';s='tq232_thomas_interview2009'
P(p,
'A historian of early modern beliefs, social relations and changing attitudes toward nature. In his interview with Alan Macfarlane, Thomas explains how a historical puzzle about astrology led him toward anthropological comparison in Religion and the Decline of Magic. He recalls Christopher Hill and Tawney as important intellectual influences, and credits Lawrence Stone with encouraging the expansion of his review of Evans-Pritchard into the 1963 history-and-anthropology essay. His Annales interest centred on Febvre’s inquiry into people’s assumptions rather than hard quantitative history. He later characterized his own work as descriptive ethnography and acknowledged that his explanation of magic’s decline remained incomplete. Machielsen and Pfeffer’s reassessment supplies a separate critical reading of the 1971 book’s categories and continuing uses. These resources and disagreements locate a historical practice without making Thomas the founder of one unified anthropological school.',
'Selected professional sections of Macfarlane’s 2009 author-posted interview read: part 1 at 32:57 and 51:15; part 2 at 16:43, 20:37, 27:26, 36:50 and 43:02. Earlier Machielsen/Pfeffer and Stone scopes retained. Full selected books and 1963 essay body unread. Evans-Pritchard lacks an individual endpoint; credit stays in prose. Hildred Geertz is not Clifford Geertz. Personal/family and community material excluded.',
sources=[S(s,'Keith Thomas, interview by Alan Macfarlane, 5 September 2009: professional-method sections.','https://www.alanmacfarlane.com/DO/filmshow/kthomastx2.htm','Part 2 specified timestamps read; part 1 https://www.alanmacfarlane.com/DO/filmshow/kthomastx1.htm at 32:57 and 51:15. Thomas’s retrospective account of resources, methods and limits, not independent correspondence or complete book reading.')],
edges=[E(p,'hill','christopher_hill',p,'Thomas identifies Hill as his main undergraduate intellectual influence and recalls the high scholarly expectations of his teaching.',s,'Part 1, 51:15:24, explicit retrospective testimony. Bounded training influence, not automatic adoption of Hill’s Marxism.',kind='influence'),E(p,'tawney','r_h_tawney',p,'Thomas identifies Tawney as a deep intellectual influence while distinguishing admiration from copying historical models.',s,'Part 2, 43:02:10; part 1 school-reading recollection supplies additional context only. No claim of fresh Tawney reading.',kind='influence'),E(p,'febvre','lucien_febvre',p,'Thomas credits the Febvre strand of Annales with encouraging inquiry into historical values and mental assumptions.',s,'Part 2, 36:50:21, explicitly distinguishes that attraction from hard quantitative Annales. Not allegiance to all Annales methods.'),E(p,'stone','lawrence_stone',p,'Thomas credits Stone with encouraging the expansion of his Evans-Pritchard review into the history-and-anthropology intervention.',s,'Part 2, 16:43:03. Concrete publication encouragement and conceptual discussion, not the source of every anthropological idea.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Professional-method timestamps specified above')],holds=['Full books, original 1963 essay and original Geertz exchange unread.'])

p='joanna_bourke';s='tq232_bourke_fearmethod2024'
P(p,
'A historian of gender, warfare, violence and emotion. Her work connects bodily experience with cultural meanings and unequal power. In her later methodological account of fear, Bourke asks what fear does: its expression follows learned conventions but can also change the feeling it articulates. She uses William Reddy’s emotives to avoid equating an emotional state with a mere report of it, while questioning a simple separation between mind and body. Narratives and bodily responses participate in social hierarchies, making historical context necessary to understanding emotion. Dismembering the Male and An Intimate History of Killing locate gender and embodied experience within war; these books’ complete arguments remain distinct from the primary methodological passages read. Her later cultural histories extend the question of how societies define and value persons rather than supplying timeless psychological laws.',
'Primary author-posted 2024 methodological paragraphs, updated 2025, read on narratives, expression and emotives; medical cases, quoted patients and community content not retained. Earlier Gresham opening, Birkbeck account and publisher descriptions retain their separate scopes. Full selected books and Reddy’s original unread; Reddy lacks an individual endpoint here. The emotional-history contribution now rests on an explicit methodological argument rather than a topic or professional label.',
sources=[S(s,'Joanna Bourke, “Mastectomy, Fear, the Body: A History,” 6 February 2024, updated 5 March 2025: methodological passages.','https://www.joannabourke.com/post/mastectomy-fear-the-body-a-history','Read methodological paragraphs beginning Burney’s attempt, Perhaps part, In contrast, To be understood, While we may argue, and What these two examples through conclusion. Only scholarship/method retained; no case narrative, patient quotation, ceremony or community content. Reddy’s emotives explicitly used; his original unread.')],
replacements=[R(p,1,p,'history_of_emotions','Examines fear as an embodied and socially patterned practice whose expression can transform feeling.',s,'Primary methodological paragraphs on fear narratives and Reddy’s emotives; distinguishes feeling, expression and their interaction.',batch=1)],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Specified methodological paragraphs only')],holds=['Reddy and Scarry lack individual endpoints; no invented link from shared terminology to Peter Stearns. Complete books unread.'])

p='caroline_walker_bynum';s='tq232_bynum_body1995'
P(p,
'A medieval historian of religious practice, gender and embodiment. Bynum investigates fundamental cultural assumptions without reducing divergent medieval voices to one consistent worldview. Her retrospective credits Chenu and Grundmann with resources for studying religious thought and women’s agency; their works are not newly read here. Holy Feast and Holy Fast treats women’s religious writing as creative practice rather than merely male ventriloquism, while her body scholarship questions a simple equation of medieval Christianity with body-hating dualism. In “Why All the Fuss about the Body?” she engages sympathetically with Butler’s performative feminism but insists on comparing thinkers within their different historical settings. The point is neither that medieval theology already held modern gender theory nor that contemporary questions are irrelevant. Particularity, physical continuity and embodied personhood remain problems that discourse alone does not exhaust.',
'2012 retrospective selected pp. 434–438 and 445–450 read; earlier selected scopes retained. Newly read 1995 body essay selected pp. 4, 7–8 and 27–30, with nn. 12, 24, 85–92. Neither complete essay nor selected books read in full. Chenu/Grundmann are named resources without graph endpoints; no invented links. Butler engagement is contextual and partly sympathetic, not a blanket dismissal. Later batch may add the specifically documented Laqueur reception once that endpoint exists.',
sources=[S(s,'Caroline Walker Bynum, “Why All the Fuss about the Body? A Medievalist’s Perspective,” Critical Inquiry 22:1 (1995): 1–33.','https://www.ias.edu/sites/default/files/Why_All_The_Fuss_About_The_Body.pdf','Author/institutional posting. Selected printed pp. 4, 7–8 and 27–30 with specified notes read: discourse, Laqueur reception, performative theory and contextual comparison. Full essay, Butler originals and cited criticism unread.')],
edges=[E(p,'butler','judith_butler',p,'Bynum uses performative feminism as a productive interlocutor for comparing historically distinct accounts of embodiment and identity.',s,'Primary 27–30, especially her explicit sympathy, distinction between identity-position/continuity, and context-to-context comparison. Neither direct medieval equivalence nor wholesale theoretical adherence.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='pp. 4, 7–8, 27–30 and specified notes'),dict(source_id='tc232_bynum_paradox',locator='Selected pp. 437–438, 445–450 extend the earlier passage scope; resources and qualifications')],holds=['Chenu and Grundmann have no individual endpoints. No wholesale anti-feminist or anti-Butler verdict. Selected monographs unread.'])

p='barbara_h_rosenwein';s='dc166_rosenwein_worrying_2002'
P(p,
'A medieval historian whose emotional-communities approach treats feelings and their expression as socially valued practices in overlapping groups. “Worrying about Emotions in History” revisits Febvre’s call for an emotional history and challenges the grand narrative of progressive restraint associated with Elias. It also criticizes limits in Peter and Carol Stearns’s emotionology, especially the relation between prescriptive sources and emotional experience. Her proposal is not a claim that representations transparently reveal inner feelings. In the later interview she questions a rigid distinction between emotional regimes and refuges; Reddy’s own answer allowing plural regimes remains beside that objection. The entry therefore presents emotional communities as a methodological intervention in an ongoing debate, not a universal replacement for every other approach. Her medieval studies and selected books supply a bibliography distinct from the passages read.',
'Earlier primary scopes retained: 2002 pp. 821–822, 824–828 and 842–845, and 2010 own interview answers pp. 255–256 with Reddy’s plural-regime answer at 243. No new full article/interview or book reading claimed. Stearns criticism is jointly credited to Peter/Carol’s framework, and not treated as proof Peter denied experience in his later interview. Febvre is a specific historiographical reference, not an exclusive genealogy.',
existing=[s,'dc166_plamper_interview_2010'],
edges=[E(p,'febvre','lucien_febvre',p,'Rosenwein returns to Febvre’s programme for studying historical emotions while reconsidering the assumptions of subsequent approaches.',s,'Previously documented primary pp. 821–822. Historiographical framing/resource; no claim of direct personal transmission or unmodified adoption.'),E(p,'stearns',p,'peter_n_stearns','Questions the source limits and premodern/modern division of Peter and Carol Stearns’s emotionology.',s,'Previously documented primary pp. 824–826 and 842–845. Carol retains joint credit without an endpoint; this is Rosenwein’s criticism, not a finding that Peter’s 2010 account excludes experience.',kind='critique')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Existing documented selected pages; exact scope preserved',fresh=False)],holds=['Reddy lacks individual endpoint; his plural-regime answer retained in biography rather than inventing a node. Complete books unread.'])

p='bruce_trigger';s='tq232_trigger_wolf1987'
P(p,
'A Canadian archaeologist and ethnohistorian who sought to make Indigenous agency central to accounts of colonial encounters. Eric Wolf’s substantive reading of Natives and Newcomers identifies two connected projects: reconsidering the national narrative and bringing historical, archaeological and anthropological evidence into the same inquiry. Wolf also describes Trigger’s objection to an ethnographic present that removes social change from interpretation. This multidisciplinary programme does not remove the criticism of its categories. Emma LaRocque calls Trigger’s foreword to Sioui an example of containment and argues that the neo cross-cultural approach can distance and Other its subjects, while acknowledging its improvement on earlier racist scholarship. The Children of Aataentsic and Natives and Newcomers remain distinct milestones. Trigger is a settler scholar whose contribution and Indigenous criticism are presented together, without placing him in an Indigenous-authored field.',
'Wolf’s 1987 methodological discussion, selected printed pp. 67–69, read; no ceremonial, narrative, kinship or community knowledge retained. Existing 1986 opening and LaRocque’s recorded PDF pp. 135–136 and 249 scopes retained. Full selected books and Wolf’s other works unread. No unsupported Wolf-to-Trigger influence claim: a substantive review is reception, not proof of transmission. Existing Indigenous-authorship rejection and chronology distinctions remain.',
sources=[S(s,'Eric R. Wolf, review of Bruce Trigger, Natives and Newcomers, Culture 7:2 (1987): 67–69.','https://doi.org/10.7202/1078970ar','Read selected methodological paragraphs on multidisciplinary evidence (67), historical critique of the ethnographic present and interest groups (68), and final assessment (69). Only scholarly arguments retained; cultural/ceremonial and community detail excluded. Full original book unread.')],
replacements=[R(p,1,p,'ethnohistory','Brings converging historical, archaeological and anthropological evidence to a reconsideration of the colonial national narrative.',s,'Wolf, selected methodological paragraphs pp. 67–69; substantive named reading, not direct verification of the full book or its data.',batch=1),R(p,2,p,'anthropology','Questions interpretations that detach ethnographic descriptions from historical change.',s,'Wolf, p. 68, paragraph describing Trigger’s criticism of a timeless ethnographic present. Attributed to a named reader, not a wholesale dismissal of all Boasian scholarship.',batch=1)],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Selected methodology on pp. 67–69 only')],holds=['Wolf has no individual endpoint; neither a review nor shared topics establishes influence. LaRocque critique remains exact; original monographs unread.'])

p='james_rodger_miller';s='tq232_miller_gelinas2010'
P(p,
'A settler historian of Indigenous–newcomer relations, residential schooling and treaties. Shingwauk’s Vision combines institutional research and interviews, but Celia Haig-Brown’s review challenges the homogenization of experiences, the balance of explanatory sympathy and the handling of resistance. Those objections qualify the national synthesis rather than disappearing behind its breadth. Claude Gélinas reads Compact, Contract, Covenant as a history of political relationships through changing treaty-making, with Indigenous purposes and perspectives included rather than treaties treated solely as instruments of dispossession. He does not claim the book creates an entirely new general interpretation; his comparison with Arthur Ray concerns the contribution of a particular synthesis, not proof of intellectual influence. These named readings distinguish Miller’s institutional and political projects while keeping community testimony and knowledge outside the entry.',
'Haig-Brown’s selected review pp. 100–101 and Gélinas’s scholarly framing/evaluation at pp. 159 and 161 read. Treaty practices, ceremonial content, oral narratives and community/family details are not retained. Full Miller books unread; no first-founder claim, no universal student experience, no namesake union. Gélinas’s comparison to Arthur Ray is mediated and Ray has no individual endpoint. Life evidence and the 2025 death notice remain separate.',
sources=[S(s,'Claude Gélinas, review of J. R. Miller, Compact, Contract, Covenant, Revue d’histoire de l’Amérique française 64:1 (2010): 159–161.','https://doi.org/10.7202/1006992ar','Read introductory scholarly framing on 159 and closing assessment on 161. Contribution is a treaty-centred political synthesis attentive to Indigenous perspectives; reviewer disclaims a wholly new general interpretation and compares its contribution with Ray. No treaty ceremony, oral content or community detail retained. Full book unread.')],
replacements=[R(p,1,p,'native_newcomer','Studies changing Indigenous–newcomer political relations through treaty-making, alongside a contested residential-school synthesis.',['tc232_miller_haigbrown',s],'Haig-Brown pp. 100–101 and Gélinas pp. 159, 161. Separate named readings; neither validates the full books or removes criticisms.',batch=1)],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Scholarly framing p. 159 and evaluation p. 161 only')],holds=['Haig-Brown, Gélinas and Arthur Ray have no individual endpoints; preserve criticism and comparison in prose rather than inventing graph nodes. Full books unread.'])
